    "question": {
      ":question/id": "84bf9751-ebb8-5e79-91db-985eccc4a329",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(-2)\\frac{y^{2}}{x}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,1)$,$(-3,-3)$,$(-1,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,1)}=(-2)\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(3)}=- \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,-3)}=(-2)\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{(-3)}=6, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-1)}=(-2)\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{(-1)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/00/000427c9d0b2e11517896d3c5ccb30c51a1d0f587df6a6e627fe6c7005649f48.png)",
