    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a2cae54f-948c-5a8e-930c-c29f5d45dbd6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(-2)x+(1)y}{3}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,-3)$,$(-1,2)$,$(-2,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-3)}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)+(1)(-3)}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=\\frac{(-2)(-1)+(1)(2)}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)+(1)(1)}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/5b/5bcdb8d9b9b463d4f9c303cd0105931f0d827fe393d13fe2bfde7c7b3c333213.png)",
